Make per-voice mute take effect while the tune plays.

Upstream reSIDfp implements SID::mute() by substituting 0 for the next value
the tune writes to the voice's control register ($D404/$D40B/$D412). A mute
therefore does nothing until the tune happens to write that register, and a
tune that writes it once at init - which is legal, and what tone.sid and
tone3.sid in tests/fixtures do - can never be muted at all while it plays.
From a listener's seat the feature looks broken: you press the button and
nothing happens.

This moves the mute to the mixing stage instead. The voice keeps running, so
its oscillator and envelope stay in step with the tune and with any
neighbouring voice that uses it for ring modulation or oscillator sync; only
its contribution to the filter input is faded out. Control-register writes
are passed through unaltered, so sync and ring-mod partners of a muted voice
sound exactly as they would unmuted, which upstream's version cannot manage.

The fade, rather than a hard zero, is there because a 6581 voice sits on a
DC pedestal of its own: dropping it in one sample steps the filter input and
the external high-pass turns that step into an audible thump, measured at
-17 dBFS peak and decaying over about 0.8 s. Spreading the change over 4096
clocks, a little over 4 ms, makes a mute silent while still being instant to
a listener.

Applies to libsidplayfp 2.6.0. Verified by scripts/verify-engine.mjs, which
measures each voice's partial in the mix while muting it live.

--- a/src/builders/residfp-builder/residfp/SID.h
+++ b/src/builders/residfp-builder/residfp/SID.h
@@ -109,6 +109,13 @@
     bool muted[3];
 
     /**
+     * Per-voice mute gain, 0..1, moving towards the flag above one step at a
+     * time so that a mute fades instead of stepping the filter input.
+     * Mutable because it is advanced from output(), which is const.
+     */
+    mutable float muteGain[3];
+
+    /**
      * Emulated nonlinearity of the envelope DAC.
      *
      * @See Dac
@@ -131,6 +138,14 @@
     void ageBusValue(unsigned int n);
 
     /**
+     * Advance one voice's mute gain by a step and return it.
+     *
+     * @param channel the voice
+     * @return the gain to apply to that voice this clock
+     */
+    float muteRamp(int channel) const;
+
+    /**
      * Get output sample.
      *
      * @return the output sample
@@ -315,11 +330,34 @@
 }
 
 RESID_INLINE
+float SID::muteRamp(int channel) const
+{
+    // 4096 clocks from silent to full, a little over 4 ms on a PAL machine.
+    const float STEP = 1.0f / 4096.0f;
+    const float target = muted[channel] ? 0.0f : 1.0f;
+    float gain = muteGain[channel];
+
+    if (gain < target)
+    {
+        gain += STEP;
+        if (gain > target) gain = target;
+    }
+    else if (gain > target)
+    {
+        gain -= STEP;
+        if (gain < target) gain = target;
+    }
+
+    muteGain[channel] = gain;
+    return gain;
+}
+
